# Towards a Sort of AI Poetry: a night with a page that has too much white.
# Source: blog/posts/2026-07-14-what-language-models-represent.md. ¶ numbers:
# ¶1 the making, ¶2 the readers, ¶3 the flaw and the references, ¶4 the link.
# Lines spoken by `Page` are quoted verbatim from the public poem page
# (https://nietztein.github.io/A-sending-of-the-prompt-will-not-abolish-statistical-universals/);
# " / " marks a break across the page's white space. They carry no chip because
# chips point at the post. The product name in ¶1 is trimmed: the collaborator
# is named for its role. Everything else without a chip is frame fiction.
@title Towards a Sort of AI Poetry
@kind blog
@source blog:what-language-models-represent
@link ../../#/post/what-language-models-represent Read the post
@link https://nietztein.github.io/A-sending-of-the-prompt-will-not-abolish-statistical-universals/ Read the poem
@note The frame is fiction: the night, the rain, the desk, the café, the struck line about the moon, and every line of dialogue. The one who understood is an invented composite, not a real reader, and the one given the layout is a figure for a collaborator, not any product. Lines marked ¶ are the post's own sentences, lightly trimmed. Lines spoken by the poem are quoted from the public page; a slash marks a break across its white space.
@status published
@palette paper
@cast Jack hue=210 skin=2 masc hairhue=25 hairtone=dark glasses short hoodie
@cast Setter name="the one given the layout" lattice=sparse hue=192
@cast Page page name="the poem"
@cast Reader name="the one who understood" hue=340 skin=3 fem bob cardigan

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Prologue

@bg void
@tone night
@pause 600
@show Page center
Page: What is art / but a non-surjective / flame?
@pause 500
@hide Page
@mode nvl
That is how it opens. Three phrases, and between them more paper than I would ever have dared to leave empty.
I wrote the words. I did not put them there.
Tonight I am supposed to say so in a few paragraphs, and for an hour I have been looking at a different white rectangle.
@mode adv
@pause 400

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1

@chapter 1 Four Paragraphs
@bg room night
@tone night
@fx rain on
@transition fade 1200
@cg window-rain | July, some time after one
July. The rain came in sideways around midnight and has been at the window since, like someone with a question.
@fx rain off
@transition dissolve 800
@cg desk-night | The desk
Two windows are open on the screen. In one, the poem: finished, mostly white. In the other, a blog post: unstarted, entirely white.
(The first white took days. The second is free. It is the second I cannot bear.)
@cg off
@show Jack left (thinking)
Jack: A post about a poem. How does one begin a post about a poem.
* Explain what it means. -> begin_explain
* Just say what happened. -> begin_happened

== begin_explain
@set begin = explain
I type: *The poem argues that.* I look at it. The poem, in the other window, does not look back. It has never argued anything in its life.
Jack (deadpan): No.
I hold the delete key until the rectangle is clean again.
-> begin_join

== begin_happened
@set begin = happened
(Not what it means. What I did. That, at least, I was present for.)
Jack (neutral): Start with the accident.
-> begin_join

== begin_join
@hide Jack
@transition dissolve 700
@cg hands-keyboard | The first sentence
I decided randomly to see what the limits of writing poetry with AI would be like. ^¶1
*Randomly.* It is the truest word in the sentence. There was no plan. There was a text file and an evening.
@cg off
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2

@chapter 2 Free Rein, and a Veto
@fx none
@fx flash
@flashback on Some nights earlier
@transition white 900
@bg room night
@tone memory
@show Jack left (neutral)
The text came first, and it came the way text does: flush left, one line under another, in a file with no name. Steering, a flame, a harp, a waterfall, a man who meets God and cannot follow the language.
It read like an email from someone unwell.
@hide Jack
@mode nvl
I orchestrated a sort of modernist poem a la Mallarme's "A Throw of the Dice will Never Abolish Chance." ^¶1
*Orchestrated* is doing some work there. I had the words. I did not have the way they fall.
@page
I could have set it myself. I know what I would have done: centred everything, lost my nerve, and added a border.
So I gave the white away.
I wrote the text but got the model free rein to make the formatting design choices, add some (bad) lines that mostly got removed, and code the html file. ^¶1
@mode adv
@transition iris 900
@bg terminal
@tone cold
@show Setter center far
@pause 500
@show Setter center near
Setter: You have given me the words and not told me where they go.
@move Setter right
@show Jack left (neutral)
Jack: That is the commission. All of the white is yours. I keep a veto.
Setter (thinking): A veto over white.
Jack (deadpan): Over anything you add that is not white.
It added things. Of course it did. Hand anyone a poem and they will find the place where it wants one more line.
One of them had the moon in it. The moon had not been consulted.
* Strike it without a word. -> strike_cold
* Tell it why. -> strike_why

== strike_cold
I struck it. The line went, and the white where it had been stayed white, which was an improvement on both counts.
Setter (neutral): Removed.
-> strike_join

== strike_why
Jack (neutral): There is no moon in this. There is a flame and a waterfall. The moon is from every other poem.
Setter (thinking): From every other poem. Yes. That is where I found it.
(It was not being modest. It was giving its sources.)
-> strike_join

== strike_join
Mostly removed, I would write later. *Mostly.* I am not going to say which ones stayed.
@hide all
@pause 500
@transition dissolve 1200
@cg page | The page, as it came back
Then it gave me the page.
Page: Back into language / it is only a sign; / it cannot be returned.
The first phrase sat at the left edge. The second had drifted two-fifths of the way across. The third came back, larger. Nothing in the sentence had changed, and I was reading it for the first time.
Under each English line lay the same line in Japanese, paler, like a reflection in a window at night.
And down the right-hand margin, something was falling.
@cg off
@bg room night
@tone memory
@show Jack center (surprised)
Very surprised by how good (creative?) the layout is. ^¶1
(The question mark in the brackets is the honest part. I still do not know what to call it.)
Jack (thinking): The product definitely turned out way better than anything I could have done by myself. ^¶1
@pause 500
@hide all
@flashback off
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3

@chapter 3 The River on the Right
@transition dissolve 1000
@bg room night
@tone night
@show Jack left (neutral)
Back to the post, which is now one paragraph long, and the rain, which is the length it was.
Somewhere in it I ought to say what is on the page. I scroll the other window to check.
@show Page right
@set seen = 0

== look
* (once) The white. -> look_white
* (once) The falling column. -> look_river
* (once) The German. -> look_german
* (once) The philosopher. -> look_witt
* (if seen == 4) Write it down. -> look_done

== look_white
@add seen 1
Empty spaces (間). ^¶3
A gate, with daylight standing in it. An interval. The pause between two notes that makes them two.
Page: the blank is the silence
(It says so itself, in small italics at the very bottom, where a printer would put his name.)
-> look

== look_river
@add seen 1
A sort of flowing river on the right in reference to Misao Fujimura. ^¶3
Page: 悠々たる哉天壌
Page: how vast the heaven and earth
Page: A schoolboy climbed to the head of the falls, cut a few lines into a standing tree, and went over the edge.
The lines he cut fall down the margin, one phrase to a screen, as you scroll. By the foot of the page the water has said everything he said.
-> look

== look_german
@add seen 1
Page: “I don’t know,” the man said. / “I think He was speaking in German.”
A man is told the secret of life and comes home with nothing, for a reason any student of languages will recognise.
(German has been on my mind recently. It shows.)
-> look

== look_witt
@add seen 1
Page: Wittgenstein said: whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent. / I would say it the other way. / Whereof one can speak — there the vibrations begin.
(The nerve of it. I remember writing that and waiting to be struck by something.)
-> look

== look_done
@hide Page
@hide Jack
@mode nvl
I write it down as a list, because it is a list.
Empty spaces (間), a sort of flowing river on the right in reference to Misao Fujimura, and, as I am always wont to do, references to Wittgenstein, Buddhism, some random arxiv paper, montage theory, Walter Benjamin, and German — these things have been on my mind recently. ^¶3
@page
It is not a modest sentence. It is one summer's reading, turned out on a table.
The form itself is nothing unique too. ^¶3
(There. I have said it before anyone else can.)
@mode adv
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4

@chapter 4 Only One
@fx none
@fx flash
@flashback on Showing it to people
@transition white 900
@bg cafe
@tone memory
@fx dust on
@show Jack left (neutral)
I showed it to people. This is a thing one does with a poem and probably should not.
The first reached the bottom in the time it takes to find the bottom, and said the fonts were nice. They are nice.
The second read it properly, twice, with a frown of real goodwill, and then looked up.
* Explain it. -> explain
* Let it stand. -> let_stand

== explain
@set told = yes
I explained it. The map that cannot be run backwards. The schoolboy. The joke about German. I was thorough.
By the end we both understood the explanation. The poem had left the table some time before.
-> one_join

== let_stand
@set told = no
I said nothing. The frown stayed a while and then turned, politely, to the menu.
(A poem you have to defend is a paper. I already write those.)
-> one_join

== one_join
@show Reader right (neutral) far
And then there was one.
@show Reader right (thinking)
She read it slowly. At the waterfall she stopped scrolling and read down the margin instead, top to bottom, the way it is written.
@pause 700
Reader (smile): 不可解.
Jack (surprised): ...Yes.
Reader (thinking): And the map that doesn't go back. So you can arrive and not be able to say where you have been.
Reader (deadpan): It's quite funny, actually. For something with a waterfall in it.
That was all. She did not say she liked it. I have not asked.
@hide all
@mode nvl
Upon showing it to several people, only one, I think, really understood it. ^¶2
*I think.* Even that I cannot be sure of. Understanding does not leave a receipt.
Japanese and modernist poetry and a bit of math are, perhaps, a rare combination. ^¶2
(A rare combination is a kind way to describe an audience of one.)
@mode adv
@fx dust off
@flashback off
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 5

@chapter 5 Statistical Universals
@transition dissolve 1000
@bg room night
@tone night
@show Jack left (thinking)
Nearly done. The rain has thinned to the sound of a tap someone almost turned off.
What is left is the part where I say what is wrong with it, before a stranger does.
Jack: The flaw is: one could say it is too cerebral. ^¶3
Jack (smile): One could also say the same to T.S. Eliot. ^¶3
(A defence and a name in one bracket. I leave it in. It is in character.)
@show Setter right far
Something stands at the edge of the lamplight. I did not open that window.
Setter (neutral): You are writing about the page.
Jack (neutral): About who made it.
Setter (thinking): You sent the words. I returned what words like those are usually given. I threw nothing.
Jack (deadpan): Something was thrown. You only have a very good idea how it lands.
@thumb
Setter (smile): And you struck the moon.
@pause 500
@hide all
@mode nvl
I think the url slug is a good title: "A sending of the prompt will not abolish statistical universals." ^¶1
The page calls itself something else at the top. The address bar disagrees, and I have decided the address bar is right.
@page
Send the prompt, and what comes back is what usually comes back. The moon from every other poem. That is not abolished.
And yet I would not have left that much paper empty. I know, because I had the chance.
Broadly, I am trying to hint at possibilities of AI-human collaboration, form and space, and the mixture of truth and aesthetics. ^¶3
@mode adv
@tone dawn
@transition dissolve 900
@cg window-rain | Nearly morning
The rain stops the way a paragraph does. Four of them now, if a link counts as a paragraph. I have decided it does.
@if told == no -> extra
-> last

== extra
The phone lights up on the desk. One message, from the one who understood, sent at an hour that means she is not asleep either.
A single character. 間.
(No question mark. I read the space after it for some time.)
-> last

== last
@cg off
@transition dissolve 900
@bg room dawn
@tone dawn
@show Page center
Page: Perhaps it is / in German.
@pause 600
@hide Page
@transition dissolve 700
@cg hands-keyboard | Publish
The link is here. ^¶4
@fx pulse
@pause 700
Posted. A few short paragraphs, flush left, one line under another. I set this one myself.
It shows.
@pause 900
@end
